WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.147 --> 00:00:01.230
My name is Loren Brichter.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:03.180
I invented pull-to-refresh.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:05.172
I worked at Apple on
the original iPhone.

00:00:05.172 --> 00:00:06.630
I wrote a programme
called Tweetie,

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:09.750
which was sold to Twitter,
and became the Twitter client

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:10.710
for the iPhone.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.450
I worked on a game
called Letterpress.

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:15.885
And now, I'm one of the
creators of the Ragmask project.

00:00:15.885 --> 00:00:17.260
And can you tell
me what that is?

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:18.705
What the Ragmask project is?

00:00:18.705 --> 00:00:21.720
The Ragmask project is a set
of extremely simple, extremely

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.300
fast-to-manufacture
plans for face masks.

00:00:28.805 --> 00:00:30.455
Why did you decide to do this?

00:00:30.455 --> 00:00:32.369
We had a sewing machine.

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:34.550
And I tried following a
few of the plans online.

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:37.070
And I'm not an experienced
sewer by any stretch

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.400
of the imagination.

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:40.820
So we just developed
something that

00:00:40.820 --> 00:00:44.990
was much simpler to create,
something that I could follow.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:46.790
Like that was the
design constraint.

00:00:46.790 --> 00:00:48.410
Could I do it?

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.190
This is one of my
first sewing projects.

00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:52.670
I guess my philosophy is, I
can't think about something

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.720
without using my hands.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:54.800
I mean, it's the same
with programming.

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.883
It's the same with fabric.

00:00:55.883 --> 00:00:57.110
It's the same with wood.

00:00:57.110 --> 00:00:58.380
It's the same with anything.

00:00:58.380 --> 00:01:00.740
So if I can get some fabric in
my hands and a sewing machine,

00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:02.198
and you basically
just like iterate

00:01:02.198 --> 00:01:03.950
over and over and
over and over again,

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:06.270
eventually good ideas
will fall out of it.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.600
And this particular
shape of mask

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.390
just happens to be
extremely easy to make.

00:01:11.390 --> 00:01:13.310
And it fits pretty well.

00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:14.310
It's not finished yet.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.940
We have like two or three
more versions coming.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:20.090
And I'm thinking of it more as
a technology product rather than

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:20.630
a garment.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.880
We're literally versioning it.

00:01:21.880 --> 00:01:24.560
We're up to version 2.2,
2.3 is coming tonight.

00:01:24.560 --> 00:01:26.030
3.0 is coming next week.

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:29.540
And they're each going to
be refined in some way.

00:01:29.540 --> 00:01:31.840
Do you have one of the
masks there, by any chance?

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:32.680
I do.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.700
I don't have one of the
ones on the website now.

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.658
But I have one of the
ones that I'm working on.

00:01:36.658 --> 00:01:39.740
We're trying to make it
even simpler to make.

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:41.523
Straps...

00:01:41.523 --> 00:01:42.440
we can't find elastic.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:43.523
Everyone's out of elastic.

00:01:43.523 --> 00:01:45.560
You can't even buy
it online anymore.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:47.090
So that's why we
call it a ragmask,

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.590
where literally,
you can make it out

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.590
of rags, dish towels,
sheets, old pillowcases.

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:55.340
So this one, where we're telling
people, it's not out yet,

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:58.400
but we basically just
put loops on the ends.

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.920
And then people can take
shoelaces, or anything else

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:01.440
they have.

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:03.740
We're going to deliver masks
without any straps at all,

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:07.090
and they're going to
supply their own straps.

00:02:07.090 --> 00:02:09.560
And it saves us a lot
of time and material.

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:10.990
Can anyone make these masks?

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.605
And what do you
need to make them?

00:02:12.605 --> 00:02:14.438
Really, all you need
is a needle and thread,

00:02:14.438 --> 00:02:17.270
and some old fabric, any fabric.

00:02:17.270 --> 00:02:19.070
A few people now have
hand-stitched them.

00:02:19.070 --> 00:02:20.330
It takes a little bit longer.

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.280
If you have a sewing machine,
it goes a lot faster.

00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:23.480
You really want to
wear any kind of mask.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.130
Even if you don't have
a needle and thread,

00:02:25.130 --> 00:02:26.630
there are tons of
designs out there.

00:02:26.630 --> 00:02:28.040
The CDC just put one up.

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:30.710
You can fold up anything
with two rubber bands

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.240
and put it behind your ears.

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:34.325
I mean, that's more
effective than nothing.

00:02:34.325 --> 00:02:37.010
How do you know that
these masks are effective?

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:38.960
A lot of the benefit
isn't necessarily

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:41.543
the filtration
ability of the mask.

00:02:41.543 --> 00:02:43.460
Because you're not
filtering the virus itself.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.060
You're filtering out
droplets, which are much,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.480
much larger than the virus.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:49.850
But you're reducing
the radius of aerosols

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:52.460
coming out of your mouth
and infecting one another.

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:54.680
We started the
project before the CDC

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.150
and the World
Health Organization

00:02:56.150 --> 00:02:57.835
were recommending
widespread mask use.

00:02:57.835 --> 00:02:59.210
So at this point
we're relatively

00:02:59.210 --> 00:03:01.505
confident that they're
effective in some way.

00:03:01.505 --> 00:03:03.780
On the wider public
perception of mask wearing,

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.030
can you talk a bit about that?

00:03:05.030 --> 00:03:06.230
Whether you think
everyone should wear them

00:03:06.230 --> 00:03:07.145
outside or not?

00:03:07.145 --> 00:03:08.720
I feel like most of the people
watching this video probably

00:03:08.720 --> 00:03:10.820
aren't wearing pants,
because everyone's at home.

00:03:10.820 --> 00:03:13.028
Like, no one can tell if
I'm wearing pants right now.

00:03:13.028 --> 00:03:15.585
But culturally, if you do
have to leave the house

00:03:15.585 --> 00:03:18.370
- which you should avoid
- you would put on pants.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:20.120
And I think the shift
that needs to happen

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:24.360
is if you put on your pants,
you should put on a mask.

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:27.260
And if you're the only
one wearing pants,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.050
that's not enough.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.717
Everyone else needs
to be wearing pants.

00:03:29.717 --> 00:03:31.477
Everyone else needs
to be wearing a mask.

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.810
You should get weird looks
if you're not wearing a mask.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.310
That's the way I think about it.

